Brief Project Description
Harmful language detection is a core task in natural language processing with direct applications
in online moderation systems. The goal of this project is to study how effectively small pretrained transformer encoder models can be fine-tuned for harmful and toxic text detection, and
whether such models exhibit unintended bias across identity groups. While large language models
have recently received significant attention, many deployed moderation systems rely on smaller
transformer-based classifiers due to their stability and ease of deployment. Prior work has demonstrated that toxicity and hate-speech classifiers may behave differently for text mentioning specific
identity groups (e.g., gender, religion, sexual orientation), even when the text itself is non-toxic [1].
Additional studies show that while larger models often achieve stronger performance on personal
insults, both large and small models struggle with categories involving social group references, including religion and discrimination [?]. In this project, we focus on fine-tuning small encoder-based
transformer models from the BERT family, which are widely used for text classification tasks. We
will fine-tune these models on harmful-text datasets and empirically evaluate their performance
using ROC-AUC, alongside subgroup-based metrics such as subgroup AUC and false negative rates
for toxic comments mentioning specific identity groups, in order to identify potential bias gaps in
model behavior.
Requirements and Assumptions
We will use publicly available pretrained model weights and datasets through the Hugging Face
ecosystem. In particular, we plan to use BERT-based model variants and the Google Jigsaw
Unintended Bias in Toxicity Classification dataset, which includes both toxicity labels and identity
annotations. After reviewing the computational resources provided by the university and described
in the project guidelines, we conclude that these resources are more than sufficient to support the
proposed experiments. All required data, model weights, and APIs are publicly accessible.
